afw-deploy

Deploy one or more DAGs to the dev Airflow sandbox via fzf picker.

Can be invoked from anywhere inside the project — the function
walks up from $PWD looking for the first directory that contains
both airflow_manager/ and pyproject.toml (typically `.../airflow/`
in the orchestration repo) and cds there before proceeding.

USAGE
  afw-deploy

WHAT IT DOES
  1. Walks up from $PWD to find the project root (a directory
     containing both airflow_manager/ and pyproject.toml) and
     cds there. Aborts if no such ancestor exists.
  2. Verifies that "uv run afw" resolves to a binary inside the
     project's .venv/ (not a stray afw on $PATH). If the venv is
     missing afw, runs "uv sync" automatically once; if afw is still
     not in the venv after that, aborts with a three-line diagnostic
     (expected venv, resolved afw, project dir). uv honours the
     project's requires-python and will fetch a managed interpreter
     when no compatible one is installed.
  3. Opens a multi-select fzf picker over *.py files in
     airflow_manager/dag_folder/. Tag DAGs with tab / shift-tab
     (ctrl-a selects all, ctrl-d deselects all); enter deploys the
     tagged set (or the highlighted row if none are tagged).
     Entries are grouped, newest first within each group:
       - locally edited (uncommitted vs HEAD)      — yellow, on top
       - touched in this branch (vs the merge-base
         / fork-point with origin/HEAD)            — green, below
       - everything else                           — default color
     Within each group, files are sorted by mtime (newest first).
  4. Deploys the selected DAG(s) in a single afw invocation (afw's
     -d/--dags option is multiple=True, so one -d is passed per DAG;
     afw stops on the first DAG that fails):

       uv run afw sandbox deploy \
         -e dev \
         -s $DBT_DATASET_PREFIX \
         -d <dag-a.py> -d <dag-b.py> …

     No -b/--bucket is passed: afw resolves the DAG bucket from the
     live Composer environment, which stays correct when that
     environment is recreated. This previously hardcoded the
     pre-Composer-3 bucket, which still exists and still accepts
     uploads — so deploys reported "Done!" while writing somewhere
     no scheduler reads, and the DAG never became effective.

  5. Waits until Composer has actually parsed the new code (see
     WAITING below), so the command returns when the DAG is live
     rather than when the upload finished.

WAITING
  afw returns as soon as the zip is in GCS; the scheduler picks it
  up on its own schedule, so there is a window where the deploy
  "succeeded" but the old DAG code is still running. After a
  successful deploy the function polls the environment's Airflow
  REST API (/api/v2/dags) until every deployed file has been
  re-parsed.

  Composer re-parses the DAG bundle on a timer (~40-80s observed)
  whether or not anything changed, so "wait for the next parse" is
  not the same question as "is my code live". Three conditions must
  all hold before the wait reports success:

    - the DAG zip's GCS generation changed (something was really
      uploaded to the bucket this environment reads),
    - the file's last_parsed_time is at or after the moment the new
      zip landed in GCS, and
    - that parse time differs from the pre-deploy snapshot.

  Exit status:
    0  every DAG re-parsed, no import errors
    2  timed out (the upload succeeded; Composer may just be slow)
    3  parsed, but the DAG reports import errors — the new code is
       broken; the Airflow UI URL is printed
    4  the zip in GCS did not change, so nothing can become
       effective (this is what a wrong-bucket deploy looks like)

  Environment variables:
    AFW_NO_WAIT=1         skip the wait entirely
    AFW_WAIT_TIMEOUT=N    seconds before giving up (default 300)
    AFW_WAIT_INTERVAL=N   seconds between polls (default 10)
    AFW_COMPOSER_ENV      environment name (default sdp-composer3)
    AFW_COMPOSER_LOCATION region (default europe-west1)
    AFW_COMPOSER_BUCKET   skip the bucket lookup

PICKER LAYOUT
  Each fzf line is a tab-separated record with hidden sort-key
  columns (group, -mtime) and a display column plus a raw-path
  column. fzf's --delimiter=$'\t' --with-nth=3 shows only the
  colored display column; the raw path is read back from the 4th
  field, so no ANSI strip-on-readback is needed. The group column is
  0 = locally edited, 1 = branch-touched, 2 = unchanged. If the working
  directory is not a git repo, or the default branch / merge-base
  can't be determined, the grouping / color-highlight pass is skipped
  and the picker still works (sorted by mtime only).

LAST SELECTION
  The last DAG(s) chosen for each project root are cached in
  ~/.cache/afw-deploy/ (one filename per line). On subsequent runs
  fzf's query is pre-filled with the first previously-selected DAG.

Requires: uv, fd, fzf, git; gcloud, curl, jq for the wait step.
